  {
    "language_code": "",
    "language_probability": 1,
    "text": "The tricks are simple, and they work. Big orders are split into small batches to stay under reporting limits. Chips are declared as spare parts or household electronics. Ownership hides behind layer after layer of companies, so a bank checking the paperwork only ever sees the first name in the chain. And Russia pays for the privilege. A chip that costs a few dollars in Texas can sell for many times that by the time it reaches Moscow.",
